## ⚡ Performance

This package is faster than Chardet.

| Package            | Accuracy | Mean per file (ms) |
|--------------------|:--------:|:------------------:|
| chardet            |  100 %   |     0.4 ms[^2]     |
| charset-normalizer |   98 %   |       0.4 ms       |

| Package            | 99th percentile | 95th percentile | 50th percentile
|
|--------------------|:---------------:|:---------------:|:---------------:|
| chardet            |   2.7 ms    |     1.0 ms      |     0.2 ms      |
| charset-normalizer |     1.9 ms      |     0.9 ms      |     0.2 ms     
|

_updated as of November 2026 using CPython 3.12, Charset-Normalizer 3.5.3,
and Chardet 7.7 inside a (libc Debian) container. The host CPU is a 13th
gen Intel mobile CPU. We'll no longer update regularly those since the
sub-ms changes aren't meaningful to anyone anymore._

> Stats are generated using 477 files using default parameters. More
details on used files, see GHA workflows.
> And yes, these results might change at any time. The dataset can be
updated to include more files.
> The actual delays heavily depends on your CPU capabilities. The factors
should remain the same.
> Chardet reports higher accuracy on its own benchmark. Chardet used our
477-file
> corpus when developing its early 7.x model weights. Charset-normalizer
scores
